<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#phoenician-alphabet">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phoenician alphabet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Phoenician script</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Phoenician alphabet is a script of 22 consonant letters, written from right to left, used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> and across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> for most of the 1st millennium BCE. Conventionally dated from about 1050 BCE, it developed from the Proto-Sinaitic script, itself derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a>, and appears essentially fully formed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram-sarcophagus">sarcophagus of Ahiram</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>. Spread by Phoenician merchants, it was adopted in the 9th or 8th century BCE by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, who added vowels, and is the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, Arabic, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> scripts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet.jpg" alt="Phoenician alphabet" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: oncenawhile · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gezer_calendar_close_up.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
